---
import HowtoLayout from '../../../layouts/HowtoLayout.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    "q": "How do I localize an image for different countries?",
    "a": "Start from one master with no words in it and room for the longest language. Add each market's headline and legal line as an editable text layer, saved as a separate copy per market. Change the person or the setting only where a market needs it, with an edit that keeps the product. Then send each version to a reviewer in that market."
  },
  {
    "q": "Should I put text in the image or add it on top?",
    "a": "Add it on top when the words must be exact or may change: headlines, offers, prices, legal lines, anything translated. Fixing a layer costs nothing. Render it into the image when the text belongs to the scene, such as a sign or a label, and proof every character, because a correction means a new run."
  },
  {
    "q": "Can AI translate the text inside an image?",
    "a": "Some engines can. Google says Nano Banana 2 can translate and localize text within an image, and its page for Nano Banana Pro warns the model may struggle with grammar, spelling, cultural nuance and idiom. Use the result as a draft. A native speaker reads it at full size before it goes anywhere."
  },
  {
    "q": "What should change in a visual from one market to another?",
    "a": "The words always change: headline, offer and any required legal line. Casting, setting and props change only when there's a reason you can write down for that market. The product, the light and the framing stay the same in every version."
  },
  {
    "q": "How do I keep localized versions organized?",
    "a": "Name every copy for its market when you save it, and file the master and all versions in one campaign. In hubStudio a campaign points at Assets Library files without copying them, and the library filters by campaign. Each market's version then goes to its reviewer through Validation, with every version kept on one thread."
  },
  {
    "q": "Do fonts support accents and Chinese characters?",
    "a": "Not all of them. A font missing a character shows a small empty box where the letter should be. Type a test line with every accent the market uses, and a full Chinese headline, then check it at the size it will run. If the font fails, use one that covers the script, or place the line as artwork exported from your design file."
  }
];
---

<HowtoLayout slug="localize-visual-for-markets" ctaLabel="Create your account" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">The words are the hard part of localizing a visual. A headline baked into the picture has to be rebuilt for every language, and translated copy usually needs more room than the English box it replaces. Plan for both before the first render.</p>

  <h2>How do you localize images for different markets?</h2>

  <p>Make one clean master with no words in it and room left for the longest language. Add each market's text as an editable layer, render text into the picture only when it belongs to the scene, and change casting or setting only where a market needs it. In hubStudio, that's the image studio, the free Image editor and Campaigns.</p>

  <p>The first decision is where the words live.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Question</th>
          <th scope="col">Text on top, as an overlay</th>
          <th scope="col">Text rendered in the image</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">What fixing one word costs</th>
          <td>Nothing: edit the layer and save again</td>
          <td>A new run, then a fresh proof</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">How exact the words are</th>
          <td>Exactly what you typed</td>
          <td>What the engine drew; read every character</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Several languages</th>
          <td>One master, one copy per language</td>
          <td>One run or one edit per language</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Brand type, prices, legal lines</th>
          <td>Yes, set from approved copy</td>
          <td>No</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Where it wins</th>
          <td>Headlines, offers, anything a reviewer may change</td>
          <td>A sign, a label or packaging that sits inside the scene</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Past two or three markets, the overlay route is easier to control. The rendered route earns its place when flat type would look pasted on.</p>

  <h2>What goes into a master you can localize?</h2>

  <p>Everything that stays the same in every market, and nothing that doesn't. The product, the light, the framing and the mood are shared. Words are not. Whether people and places travel depends on the market.</p>

  <p>Leave room for the text. Translated copy usually needs more space than the English it came from: plan the headline area for French or German, not for English. Chinese needs the opposite caution. It often takes fewer characters, but each one is taller and more detailed, so it needs height and a size large enough to read. Don't shrink the box because the line got shorter.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The W3C's internationalization guidance says text translated from English or Chinese "will typically be longer in the translation than the original," and that Chinese, Japanese and Korean often need more height than Latin type even when they use fewer characters.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: W3C Internationalization, "Text size in translation," updated February 2016, standards-body guidance. https://www.w3.org/International/articles/article-text-size</p>

  <p>The same page puts the layout rule plainly.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">"Be especially careful about fitting text snugly into graphic designs. Separate presentation and content, so that font sizes, line heights, etc. can be easily adapted for translated text."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: W3C Internationalization, "Text size in translation," updated February 2016, standards-body guidance. https://www.w3.org/International/articles/article-text-size</p>

  <p>In the image studio, render the master with the Text to image job, or with Edit an image when you start from your own product photo. Ask for empty space by name.</p>

  <div class="prompt">
    <p class="prompt__label">Prompt example: a textless master</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">A ceramic pour-over coffee set on a pale limestone counter, morning window light from the left, one soft shadow falling right. Keep the product sharp and centered on the lower right third. Leave the upper left half as calm, even, out-of-focus wall for a headline added later, with room for two lines. No text, no letters, no signage, no logos anywhere in the frame.</p>
  </div>

  <p>The price shows before every run, and a failed run isn't charged.</p>

  <h2>What should change in a visual from one market to the next?</h2>

  <p>Start from what stays. Sort every element before anything is rendered, and keep the list with the brief.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Element</th>
          <th scope="col">Keep or change</th>
          <th scope="col">How</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Product</th>
          <td>Keep</td>
          <td>Never regenerated per market; it stays from the master</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Light, framing, palette</th>
          <td>Keep</td>
          <td>Shared by every version</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Headline and offer copy</th>
          <td>Change</td>
          <td>Overlay layer per language, from the approved copy deck</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Legal line or required label</th>
          <td>Change where a market requires it</td>
          <td>Overlay from the legal team's approved wording</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Casting</th>
          <td>Change only if the market needs it</td>
          <td>Edit an image with source pictures</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Setting and props</th>
          <td>Change only if the market needs it</td>
          <td>Edit an image, product kept</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Text inside the scene (a sign, a label)</th>
          <td>Change per language</td>
          <td>Rendered in, then proofed by a native reader</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Write the reason next to every "change." When nobody can give one, the element stays as it is in the master.</p>

  <h2>How do you add each market's text as a layer you can edit?</h2>

  <p>Open the master in the Image editor. It's free and runs in your browser; you can open it from the image studio, History or the Assets Library.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Click Add text, or double-click the picture where the line should go, then type the first market's copy.</li>
    <li>Pick the font and size, then bold, italic or underline and the alignment.</li>
    <li>Set the color. A dark outline or a soft shadow keeps light words readable on a light picture; a highlight behind the words works on a busy one.</li>
    <li>Place the brand logo from the Picture panel, in one of nine positions.</li>
    <li>Open Save, type a name that says the market (for example, "spring master FR 4x5"), and pick Save a copy in the Assets Library. The master stays as it was.</li>
    <li>Go back to the master and repeat for the next language.</li>
  </ul>

  <figure>
    <img src="/Images/help/image-editor-draw.webp" alt="The Image editor with a caption reading New season and a red arrow on the picture, the Draw panel open on the selected arrow" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>A caption stays a separate piece, movable and editable, until you save.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Each caption stays a separate piece until you save, so you can move it, restyle it or rewrite it, with up to 60 steps of undo. Before saving a copy meant for a network, the Social panel checks whether the words are large enough to read on a phone and whether anything sits under the network's buttons.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/Images/help/image-editor-save.webp" alt="The Save the picture panel: name, format, quality, size, then Save a copy in the Assets Library, Save as a new version and Download" width="1440" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>Name each copy for its market, then save it next to the master.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Brand typefaces need one workaround. The editor sets type from its own font list, so when a line must sit in the exact brand font, export it from the design file as a transparent PNG and place it like a logo. The <a href="/resources/how-to/readable-text-in-ai-images">readable text guide</a>, one of the <a href="/resources/how-to">how-to guides</a>, covers that method in more detail. For ads on Facebook and Instagram, the <a href="/solutions/platforms/meta">Meta platform page</a> covers production in the formats Meta serves.</p>

  <h2>When should the text be rendered into the image?</h2>

  <p>When the words belong to the scene. A shop sign, a bottle label or a printed bag looks real when the engine draws it with the light and the surface, and pasted on when it's typed over the top. The help center notes that the ChatGPT Image engines in the app write legible text inside the picture.</p>

  <p>Two app features help. In Skills, the Catalog holds a skill called Legible text inside an image, plus an Avoid list for hands, text artifacts and watermarks. Add them to My skills and Improve with AI follows them each time it rewrites a prompt.</p>

  <p>Some engines can also translate words already in a picture. Their makers say where that goes wrong.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google says Nano Banana 2 can "translate and localize text within an image." Its model page for Nano Banana Pro adds that the model "may struggle with grammar, spelling, cultural nuances, or idiomatic phrases."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google launch post, February 26, 2026, and Google DeepMind model page, read October 2026, the maker's own claim and caveat. https://deepmind.google/models/gemini-image/pro/</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">OpenAI's image generation guide says its GPT Image models "can still struggle with precise text placement and clarity."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: OpenAI image generation guide, read October 2026, the maker's stated limitations. https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generation</p>

  <p>So treat a rendered translation as a draft. Run it with the Edit an image job and a prompt that changes the words and nothing else.</p>

  <div class="prompt">
    <p class="prompt__label">Prompt example: a label translated in place</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">Replace the label text "Morning Blend" with "Mélange du matin". Keep the same typeface style, size, color, curve and position on the bag. Change nothing else in the image.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Then a native speaker reads every character at the size the picture will run: accents, apostrophes, line breaks. One wrong letter means another run.</p>

  <h2>How do you change the person or the setting and keep the product?</h2>

  <p>Use Edit an image, not a new render. Upload the master as the first source picture, add reference pictures for the person or the place (up to four sources, depending on the engine), and describe only the change.</p>

  <div class="prompt">
    <p class="prompt__label">Prompt example: a setting changed for one market</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">Keep the coffee set exactly as it is in the first image: same shape, glaze, color, size and position. Replace the limestone counter and wall with a narrow wooden kitchen counter by a tiled wall, as in the second image. Keep the morning light from the left and the empty upper left area. No text.</p>
  </div>

  <p>On the ChatGPT Image engines, an optional mask goes further: its transparent areas mark what may change in the first image, and everything else stays as it is. The Precise image edits skill in the Catalog shapes that kind of instruction. For a person who must look the same across a series, the <a href="/resources/how-to/consistent-character-ai-images-video">consistent character guide</a> shows the method and its limits.</p>

  <p>Check the product after every edit, against the master, at full size.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">OpenAI's guide also says its GPT Image models "may occasionally struggle to maintain visual consistency for recurring characters or brand elements across multiple generations."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: OpenAI image generation guide, read October 2026, the maker's stated limitations. https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/image-generation</p>

  <h2>Which legal lines change from one market to another?</h2>

  <p>Language rules, retouching labels and the wording of a price claim all vary by country, and they touch the layout. France is the clearest case for the text layer.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">France's 1994 language law makes French obligatory in written, spoken and audiovisual advertising, and where a translation runs beside it, the French must be "aussi lisible, audible ou intelligible" as the foreign text.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: French Law 94-665 of August 4, 1994, Articles 2 and 4, statute text on Legifrance, read October 10, 2026.</p>

  <p>That's a sizing decision as much as a legal one. Design the French layer at the same weight as the English, not as a small line underneath.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/resources/insights/europe-campaign-localization">article on localizing one campaign for Europe</a> sets out what France, Germany, Spain and Italy each ask of a campaign, from the retouched-photo label to the 30-day prior price on a discount. Headlines that need rewriting rather than translating belong to <a href="/resources/insights/transcreation-as-a-production-line">transcreation, which has its own article</a>. This guide describes production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h2>How do you keep every market's version organized and approved?</h2>

  <p>Put the whole launch in one campaign. In the app, a campaign keeps one launch's files under one name and an optional brief. It points at files in the Assets Library and copies nothing, so the master and every market's copy sit side by side. Pick the campaign under Campaign in the image studio before you render, and every picture from that page joins it. The library's Campaign filter then shows the whole set.</p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/Images/help/campaigns-page.webp" alt="A campaign's page: the band with All campaigns, Add assets and Delete the campaign, the name and the brief, the tiles by type, the Name and brief card, and In this campaign with four assets" width="1440" height="1100" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>One campaign holds the master and every market's version.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Send each market's version to that market's reviewer through Validation: a teammate, or one of the client's people when the work is made for a client. They validate it or send it back with a comment, and every version stays on one thread. The app runs in English, French and Chinese, and each person picks their own, so the reviewer in Paris or Shanghai can work in their language. Prompts and files stay in the language they were written in.</p>

  <p><a href="/app/campaigns">Campaigns</a>, Validation and the <a href="/app/image-tools">Image editor</a> cost nothing to use. When a launch runs to dozens of formats and languages, the studio can make the set with you through its <a href="/services/design/ad-creative">ad creative design service</a>.</p>

  <h2>Will your font show accents and Chinese characters?</h2>

  <p>Check before you save, every time. A font that lacks a character shows a small empty box in its place, nicknamed tofu. The project behind Google's Noto fonts took its name from the fix: "no more tofu."</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Type a test line with the accents each market uses: é, è, ç and à for French; ä, ö, ü and ß for German; ñ and the opening question mark for Spanish.</li>
    <li>For Chinese, type a full headline, not one character, and look for empty boxes anywhere in the line.</li>
    <li>Check the line at the size it will run on a phone.</li>
    <li>If the font fails, set the line in a font that covers the script, or place it as artwork exported from the design file.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What should you check before a localized version ships?</h2>

  <p>Run this on every market's file, not just the first one.</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Product identical to the master: shape, color, label, proportions.</li>
    <li>Every word matches the approved copy deck for that market.</li>
    <li>Accents, punctuation and characters render in the chosen font.</li>
    <li>Rendered text read character by character by a native speaker.</li>
    <li>Text clear of the network's buttons and readable on a phone.</li>
    <li>Required legal line or label present, from the approved wording.</li>
    <li>File named for its market and filed in the launch's campaign.</li>
    <li>Sent through Validation to that market's reviewer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions about localizing images for different markets</h2>

  <h3>How do I localize an image for different countries?</h3>

  <p>Start from one master with no words in it and room for the longest language. Add each market's headline and legal line as an editable text layer, saved as a separate copy per market. Change the person or the setting only where a market needs it, with an edit that keeps the product. Then send each version to a reviewer in that market.</p>

  <h3>Should I put text in the image or add it on top?</h3>

  <p>Add it on top when the words must be exact or may change: headlines, offers, prices, legal lines, anything translated. Fixing a layer costs nothing. Render it into the image when the text belongs to the scene, such as a sign or a label, and proof every character, because a correction means a new run.</p>

  <h3>Can AI translate the text inside an image?</h3>

  <p>Some engines can. Google says Nano Banana 2 can translate and localize text within an image, and its page for Nano Banana Pro warns the model may struggle with grammar, spelling, cultural nuance and idiom. Use the result as a draft. A native speaker reads it at full size before it goes anywhere.</p>

  <h3>What should change in a visual from one market to another?</h3>

  <p>The words always change: headline, offer and any required legal line. Casting, setting and props change only when there's a reason you can write down for that market. The product, the light and the framing stay the same in every version.</p>

  <h3>How do I keep localized versions organized?</h3>

  <p>Name every copy for its market when you save it, and file the master and all versions in one campaign. In hubStudio a campaign points at Assets Library files without copying them, and the library filters by campaign. Each market's version then goes to its reviewer through Validation, with every version kept on one thread.</p>

  <h3>Do fonts support accents and Chinese characters?</h3>

  <p>Not all of them. A font missing a character shows a small empty box where the letter should be. Type a test line with every accent the market uses, and a full Chinese headline, then check it at the size it will run. If the font fails, use one that covers the script, or place the line as artwork exported from your design file.</p>
</HowtoLayout>
